<li about="#cite_note-AllanAllan2005-172-176-69" id="cite_note-AllanAllan2005-172-176-69" data-mw-footnote-number="69"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBlU"><a href="#cite_ref-AllanAllan2005-172-176_69-0" id="mwBlY"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBlc">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-AllanAllan2005-172-176_69-1" id="mwBlg"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBlk">2</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-AllanAllan2005-172-176_69-2" id="mwBlo"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBls">3</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-AllanAllan2005-172-176-69" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt308" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" id="mwBlw" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><cite id="CITEREFKenneth_AllanKenneth_D._Allan2005" class="citation book cs1">Kenneth Allan; Kenneth D. Allan (2 November 2005). <span class="id-lock-registration" title="Free registration required" id="mwBl0"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://archive.org/details/explorationsincl00alla/page/172" class="external text" id="mwBl4"><i id="mwBl8">Explorations in Classical Sociological Theory: Seeing the Social World</i></a></span>. Pine Forge Press. pp.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBmA"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://archive.org/details/explorationsincl00alla/page/172" class="external text" id="mwBmE">172–76</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBmI">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBmM"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-1-4129-0572-5" title="Special:BookSources/978-1-4129-0572-5" id="mwBmQ"><bdi id="mwBmU">978-1-4129-0572-5</bdi></a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=Explorations+in+Classical+Sociological+Theory%3A+Seeing+the+Social+World&amp;rft.pages=172-76&amp;rft.pub=Pine+Forge+Press&amp;rft.date=2005-11-02&amp;rft.isbn=978-1-4129-0572-5&amp;rft.au=Kenneth+Allan&amp;rft.au=Kenneth+D.+Allan&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Farchive.org%2Fdetails%2Fexplorationsincl00alla%2Fpage%2F172&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ABureaucracy" class="Z3988" id="mwBmY"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-70" id="cite_note-70" data-mw-footnote-number="70"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBmc"><a href="#cite_ref-70" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwBmg"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBmk">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-70" class="mw-reference-text reference-text">
<link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt237" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBmo" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite book\n ","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last1":{"wt":"Weber"},"first1":{"wt":"Max"},"author-link1":{"wt":"Max Weber"},"editor-last1":{"wt":"Waters"},"editor-first1":{"wt":"Tony"},"editor-last2":{"wt":"Waters"},"editor-first2":{"wt":"Dagmar"},"translator-last1":{"wt":"Elbers"},"translator-first1":{"wt":"Benjamin"},"translator-last2":{"wt":"Waters"},"translator-first2":{"wt":"Tony"},"translator-last3":{"wt":"Waters"},"translator-first3":{"wt":"Dagmar"},"date":{"wt":"8 April 2015"},"chapter":{"wt":"&apos;The Nature, Conditions, and Development of Bureaucratic {{lang | de | [[Authority (sociology) | Herrschaft]]}}&apos; by Max Weber"},"title":{"wt":"Weber&apos;s Rationalism and Modern Society: New Translations on Politics, Bureaucracy, and Social Stratification"},"url":{"wt":"https://books.google.com/books?id=FDmDDAAAQBAJ"},"publication-place":{"wt":"Basingstoke"},"publisher":{"wt":"Palgrave Macmillan"},"page":{"wt":"76"},"isbn":{"wt":"9781137365866"},"access-date":{"wt":"18 August 2024"},"quote":{"wt":"1. A rigid division of labor is in place for the purpose of performing regular daily tasks as official duties in the functioning of the bureaucratically governed system.&lt;br>2. In order to fulfill these duties the necessary chains of command are firmly established and divided up [among the {{lang | de | Beamte}}], and their capacity to coerce (physical, sacred, or other) is firmly restricted by regulation.&lt;br>3. Regular and continuous fulfillment of these assigned duties, and the execution of respective rights is systematically secured by hiring people with certified qualifications.&lt;br>&lt;br>These three aspects constitute the essence of bureaucratic administration of a &apos;&apos;Behoerde&apos;&apos; in the public sector."}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFWeber2015" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt237"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Weber" title="Max Weber" id="mwBms">Weber, Max</a> (8 April 2015). "<span class="cs1-kern-left" id="mwBmw"></span>'The Nature, Conditions, and Development of Bureaucratic <span title="German-language text" id="mwBm0"><i lang="de" id="mwBm4"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authority_(sociology)" title="Authority (sociology)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBm8"> Herrschaft</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_German-language_text" id="mwBnA"/>' by Max Weber". In Waters, Tony; Waters, Dagmar (eds.). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=FDmDDAAAQBAJ" class="external text" id="mwBnE"><i id="mwBnI">Weber's Rationalism and Modern Society: New Translations on Politics, Bureaucracy, and Social Stratification</i></a>. Translated by Elbers, Benjamin; Waters, Tony; Waters, Dagmar. Basingstoke: Palgrave Macmillan. p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBnM"> </span>76. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBnQ">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBnU"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/9781137365866" title="Special:BookSources/9781137365866" id="mwBnY"><bdi id="mwBnc">9781137365866</bdi></a><span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwBng">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwBnk">18 August</span> 2024</span>. <q id="mwBno">1. A rigid division of labor is in place for the purpose of performing regular daily tasks as official duties in the functioning of the bureaucratically governed system.<br id="mwBns"/>2. In order to fulfill these duties the necessary chains of command are firmly established and divided up [among the <span title="German-language text" id="mwBnw"><i lang="de" id="mwBn0">Beamte</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_German-language_text" id="mwBn4"/>], and their capacity to coerce (physical, sacred, or other) is firmly restricted by regulation.<br id="mwBn8"/>3. Regular and continuous fulfillment of these assigned duties, and the execution of respective rights is systematically secured by hiring people with certified qualifications.<br id="mwBoA"/><br id="mwBoE"/>These three aspects constitute the essence of bureaucratic administration of a <i id="mwBoI">Behoerde</i> in the public sector.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=bookitem&amp;rft.atitle=%27The+Nature%2C+Conditions%2C+and+Development+of+Bureaucratic+%3Cspan+title%3D%22German-language+text%22%3E%3Ci+lang%3D%22de%22%3EHerrschaft%3C%2Fi%3E%3C%2Fspan%3ECategory%3AArticles+containing+German-language+text%27+by+Max+Weber&amp;rft.btitle=Weber%27s+Rationalism+and+Modern+Society%3A+New+Translations+on+Politics%2C+Bureaucracy%2C+and+Social+Stratification&amp;rft.place=Basingstoke&amp;rft.pages=76&amp;rft.pub=Palgrave+Macmillan&amp;rft.date=2015-04-08&amp;rft.isbn=9781137365866&amp;rft.aulast=Weber&amp;rft.aufirst=Max&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fbooks.google.com%2Fbooks%3Fid%3DFDmDDAAAQBAJ&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3ABureaucracy" class="Z3988" about="#mwt237" id="mwBoM"></span> 
</span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-71" id="cite_note-71" data-mw-footnote-number="71"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBoQ"><a href="#cite_ref-71" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwBoU"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBoY">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-71" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=_c3Mcnh8hCgC&amp;pg=PA19" class="external text" id="mwBoc">Max Weber (2015) extract</a>, books.google.ca; accessed 30 August 2015.</span></li>
